            <ArticleTitle>Assessing somatic, psychosocial, and spiritual distress of patients with advanced cancer: development of the Advanced Cancer Patients' Distress Scale.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>339-46</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1049909112453640</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">For adequate distress assessment in palliative care, we developed a screening evaluation tool.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Proven methods of scale construction led to a 53-item pilot form of the Advanced Cancer Patients' Distress Scale (ACPDS). We used Hornheide Questionnaire (HQ), Palliative Outcome Scale (POS), and Minimal Documentation System (MIDOS) for validation. Advanced cancer patients (N = 168) from 3 centers for palliative medicine (aged 23-89, 51% female) filled out the questionnaire.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">With a principal component analysis (PCA), we extracted 5 distress scales (emotional reactions/physical restrictions, communication deficits, negative social reactions, pain, and gastrointestinal symptoms). Internal consistencies varied between medium (.52) and very good (.88). Positive validity scores were found.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Using the ACPDS may help to identify needs for palliative care interventions and enhance the quality of palliative care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
